# A Heroic Call on Independence Day
Tamil title: விடுதலைத் திருநாளில் வீரமுழக்கம்

Speech delivered by Arignar Anna, Chief Minister of Tamil Nadu, on Independence Day, 15 August 1967:

People of the country! Friends!

Today we celebrate the sacred day of liberation. At this moment, I wish to join you and share in the joy and awakening this day brings.

The festival we celebrate today, the festival of independence, is a golden day honoured not only by us here but by the peoples of many countries throughout the world.

Today is the fifteenth of August. It is praised, and rightly so, as the blessed day on which India gained freedom from British domination and proudly raised the flag of independence; the day on which we achieved self-rule and won the struggle for our rights.

In the pages of history, this day has been inscribed as one of unique distinction.

Poets welcome it with stirring verse.

This is a sacred day, without doubt; a golden day, as none will deny. It proclaims that the world has recognised our rights. We understand this and rejoice in our hearts.

This fifteenth of August fills our hearts with memories of all those who served, each in their own way, so that we might cast off the chains of slavery, remove foreign government and domination, and secure the right to govern ourselves. It reminds us of the suffering and losses they accepted and the precious lives they gave, inspiring us to offer a warrior’s salute to those great heroes. It also gives us the uplifting awareness that we are their heirs, generation after generation.

The imperialists trumpeted: “Recover their lost government, will they? Where do they possess the strength? Unite and oppose us, will they?” They imagined that freedom fighters would flee in terror at the sound of gunfire and that the mention of prison would bewilder their minds.

But the flame of courage blazed high. The uprising for rights assumed the form of a storm. Those who had lain bent stood upright. New light appeared even in eyes that had grown dim. Fear vanished. The arrogant saw a new nation arise before them. “Is this India?” they asked. The heroes declared: “We shall fear nothing. We shall not retaliate by violence. However much you torment and torture us, we shall endure. Our country belongs to us; our government must be ours. That cry will drown even the sound of the cannon sent to subdue us.” The British asked, “Is this India?” “Yes,” Mahatma Gandhi, noble among the world’s people, replied, “this is an awakened India, an India risen with vigour, a pure nation prepared to pay any price for freedom.” This sacred day was obtained through the moral path he showed in those times.

For all of us, this is a day that recalls those events and teaches their lesson. We must reflect today upon all the ways in which the freedom fighters then imagined the country would be transformed and elevated when this day arrived.

Independence came, and we live with the right to say that we govern ourselves. We live, but do we truly live? This is the question we must ask ourselves: do we live?

There is a great difference between living and merely existing. Most among us today are in a condition where we can say only that we somehow exist, not one in which we can joyfully declare that we live.

We see the flag of independence flying in radiant splendour. We hear sweet music, conduct parades, and announce to the world that this is our Independence Day. But do we live? Has life blossomed into a good life, a new life, a full life?

Only when poverty and ignorance have disappeared will we earn the right to say that we live.

Only the day on which deprivation and insufficiency disappear will become the new day when we gain life.

Only the day when difference and division vanish and the condition arises in which all are one will be a sacred day in life. Only when people neither degrade others nor fall at another’s feet, neither exploit nor are exploited, but receive the fruits of their labour and have smiles blossom upon their faces, will we obtain the condition in which we may say that we live.

Until we reach that condition, we can say only that we exist. We have no right to claim that we live.

Liberated countries not only live; they have gained the power and resources to enable other countries to live as well. We see this and walk in search of the true form of life.

In the days before independence, we lay trapped in poverty and ignorance. Even then, the cultured people of the world did not regard us with contempt. They showed sympathy and said: “Poor people! It is indeed a land filled with resources, a soil that yields gold. Yet what can they do? British domination has driven that country’s people into misery.” They wished us freedom and extended their goodwill.

But when they see poverty, ignorance, difference, and division continuing even after liberation, what will they think and say? Whom will they identify as the cause of this condition? Are we not governing ourselves? Have we not become responsible for both our advancement and our decline?

At first, the sacred day of liberation called the fifteenth of August helped give us joy, awakening, and heroic feeling. Twenty years have now passed. Today’s fifteenth of August has become a day that must give us a sense of responsibility. We vowed that we would obtain self-rule and establish good government. Are we advancing in the effort required for it? Today’s fifteenth of August must give us the responsibility to think and act upon what must be done to achieve that ideal.

Two thousand years ago, the great Valluvar declared that a country is one free from devastating hunger, unceasing disease, and destructive enmity.

Twenty years have rolled by. Year after year we hold festivals and sing verse. Have we succeeded in creating the “country” described by Valluvar? That is the question before us today.

It would be a grave error to avoid raising this question for fear that it might spoil the joy of the celebration.

I say that this question must be raised not to make us weary, disheartened, or broken in spirit, but because it is necessary if we are to acquire the resolve to act more effectively.

This question lies beyond political parties. Yes, it is a question that must arise from the hearts of the country’s people.

Nature caresses us while deprivation pours down upon us; it cannot be concealed.

Resources increase, yet find their way into a few small enclosures; that cannot be concealed either.

The population grows while hardship writhes. We must acknowledge it.

Shortages threaten us and rising prices toss us about. Evils such as black-market trade, plunderous profit, illicit methods, and adulteration ruin the country. The flag flies with pageantry while these cruelties writhe beside it, asking, “Who will suppress us?”

On this day, we must understand the condition of the country. Awareness must be woven into the joy brought by Independence Day. Only then will the country attain the good condition for which the elders won freedom, and only then will we taste its fruit.

The desert must become a grove, and green parrots must sing there. Such poetry was not given merely to remain poetry forever. Poems like these were given so that all of us might gain confidence that the vision could be put into practice.

On this day, we must acquire that confidence.

We must not stop with the joyful declaration that we have gained independence and the strength to protect it. We must walk with determination, enthusiasm, and hope toward the ideal that will allow us proudly to celebrate not only that we gained freedom, but that we gained a life of well-being.

We were able to free ourselves from the grip of a vast empire. The ability to do so existed within us. We must also gain the conviction that this same ability should be used to remove the poverty, ignorance, discrimination, and division within this country.

Are those who raise the flag, conduct splendid celebrations, and honour the freedom fighters alone sufficient to fulfil that ideal?

The farmer who continues ploughing without tiring even when the sun has reached its zenith is also needed.

So is the worker who engages in many trades and produces goods.

So are the teachers who open the eyes of knowledge for boys and girls.

So are those in the medical profession who serve to remove suffering.

So are the engineers who restrain wild floods, use water resources to increase the fertility of the land, build settlements, and establish order.

So are the officials who work in the many branches of the country’s administration.

The splendour of independence and the fruit of liberation reside in their ability, enthusiasm for action, and success.

They are the guardians of independence. I salute them.

This sacred day is fitting for offering salutations, greetings, honour, and praise not only to the soldiers who safeguard the country recovered by noble people so that none may seize it today, but also to the workers of action who labour every day to improve its condition.

It should be a day on which we see an upright stride, radiant eyes, and smiling faces.

More than all these, it is a blessed day on which we must understand our duty and gain the resolve to engage in the work that enables the country to live.

All our individual likes and dislikes, rights and privileges, must be made to support the common duty that the country must flourish. Our methods must be shaped accordingly.

Everyone must acquire the understanding that our own labour is necessary for the country to live.

We must never think of anyone: “What can this person accomplish? Is he suited to this task? What ability does he possess?” Such a thought would harm the very life of the country.

We are all people of this country. The country will be exactly as all of us are. This is a truth everyone can accept.

The country’s possession is the combined total of all our knowledge and ability. A country is not merely a map; it is the collective feeling of the people who live there.

The common welfare of the country can rise only to the extent that each of us contributes our knowledge and ability to it.

This is a day on which every person must think about what we should do, and what offering we should make, so that the world may praise the country for having gained a good life through liberation.

Friends, this is not merely a day for rejoicing. It is a day for taking account. What have we contributed, and how much, so that the country might flourish after independence? What must we contribute from now on, and how much? This is a day for calculating that account.

The first fifteenth of August was the day an account was settled: the account between imperialism and us. It was the day a new account was opened.

Twenty years later, today’s fifteenth of August is a day for reviewing the account: a day for examining how and to what extent our words and actions have brought us a life of well-being since independence.

On this day, I join you in gaining joy and inspiration.

I gain confidence that the independence won with the aid of your knowledge and ability will be cherished and protected.

I call upon you to come and labour so that all the country’s people may receive the fruits of independence in good measure. I see you in my mind’s eye, salute your determination, and bow to you. I think of the heights the country will attain with the aid of your incomparable ability. Before me I see a flower garden of smiles. I see a beautiful future. This sacred day is the blessed day that will prepare the way for us to behold that golden age.

May you prosper! May our country prosper!

---NOTES---
The Tamil OCR source is preserved verbatim in the bilingual output. Clear contextual readings used only in English include “அணவபுரியினர்” as the arrogant or imperial rulers, “மீரங்கிச் சத்தம்” as cannon fire, the heavily damaged line after “இயற்கை கொஞ்சுகிறது” as “மறைப்பதற்கில்லை,” and “கரண்டப்படாமல்” as “without being exploited.” The quoted definition of a country is translated from the Tirukkural as it appears in the speech rather than replaced with an external edition. Political, economic, social, biographical, literary, and historical assertions are translated in the source voice and have not been independently verified.
